# 19 places read "a holder died" as "there is nothing here": the silent lock-poison batch, a hard-zero gate, and a matrix that caught its own blind spot (2026-10-05)

== 1. What this batch changes

The previous batch (`docs/verification/lock-poison-consolidation-2026-10-05.log`) dealt with locks that *panic* on
a poisoned guard: it installed `util::shared_guard` and moved 28 sites onto `lock_or_recover()` /
`read_or_recover()` / `write_or_recover()`. That fixed the crash and left behind the worse half of the same idiom
family: sites that swallow the poison error and carry on as if the data were simply absent. `.lock().ok()` does not
mean "best effort"; after a panic elsewhere in the process it means "the value is gone", forever, because poisoning
is a sticky flag on the lock rather than a property of one acquisition.

19 sites in 14 files did that. Every one of them is on a path that keeps running after a panic: the live prompt id
that stamps streaming updates, four remote configuration tiers, the telemetry mode gate, the doom-loop signal
collector, the failed-response replay capture, the pager's per-directory git cache, and one error tee in the tool
actor. All 19 now read through recovery, and a writer is never left behind when its reader is converted -- one lock,
one policy.

A new gate holds the line: `scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py`, with its own fixtures, wired into
`.github/workflows/ci.yml` and into the `gates=()` mirror in `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`. It is the 44th gate in
the host sweep. Unlike the ratchet gates around it it has **no baseline file and no test carve-out**: the tolerated
count is zero, in production code and in test code alike.

Diffstat, tracked files: 21 files, +577 / -137, plus three new files (`scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py`,
`scripts/ci/test-check-lock-poison.py`, `crates/codegen/xai-grok-test-support/src/poison.rs`).

