== 3. The rule, and where it deliberately stops

The gate matches one shape: a *synchronous* lock acquisition whose error is discarded by `.ok()`. It does not match
the shapes that are decisions rather than accidents, and that boundary is asserted by fixture rather than asserted
in prose:

  * `try_lock()` -- the whole point of the call is that it may fail, and it also fails when the lock is merely busy;
  * `if let Ok(guard) = lock.lock()` and `let Ok(g) = .. else { return }` -- an explicit branch on the error;
  * `.read().await.ok()` on an async lock -- `tokio::sync` locks have no poisoning at all, so there is nothing to
    lose;
  * `read(&mut buf)` on a `&str` -- same spelling, entirely different method, which is why the receiver and the
    argument list both have to be read before a site is reported.

Two honest limits belong in this log rather than in a footnote. First, an equivalent-but-differently-spelled
swallow is out of reach for any regex: HEAD's `doom_loop::take()` spelled it `match self.inner.lock() { Ok(..) =>
.., Err(_) => Vec::new() }`, which is the same lie in different clothes. The gate cannot see it; test M3 exists
because of it. Second, the branched-on acquisitions the gate allows are the same class one level down -- a dropped
write instead of an invented absence -- and they are an open row, not a claim of this batch. That row needed a
number, and a number anyone can only arrive at by hand once is not evidence, so the gate counts them itself:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py --inventory | head -3
      if-let-Ok      prod-drop   14  prod-hold    0  test-drop    1  test-hold    0
      let-Ok-else    prod-drop    4  prod-hold    0  test-drop    0  test-hold    0
      match-Err-arm  prod-drop    0  prod-hold    9  test-drop    0  test-hold    2

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py --inventory | tail -1
    lock-poison inventory: 30 site(s) branch on the poison error instead of reading through it -- 19 drop the guard,
    11 take it back with into_inner(); 27 of them in production code. Allowed by design; a reviewer decides per lock.

The split is the useful part: an `if let Ok(guard) = ..lock()` or `let Ok(..) = .. else` binds nothing on the failure
path, so the value is unreachable there by construction (19 sites, 18 of them in production code), while every
`match ..lock()` arm in production already takes the guard back -- `status_line.rs:172` is the model, warning first
and then using the last value, with a comment saying why one optional id cannot be left half-written by a panic
elsewhere. An earlier draft of this log and of the open row carried **41** for that population. 41 cannot be
reproduced by anything in the repository, so it has been replaced everywhere with the figure this command prints.
The inventory can under-count and cannot over-count: `match-Err-arm` requires no brace between the `match` head and
the `Err`, so an `Err` arm written after an arm with a block body is invisible to it.

== 4. What each site actually did before the fix

Read off `git show HEAD:<file>`, not off the new code. "Permanent" below means the condition never heals: the lock
stays poisoned until the process exits, so the first panic anywhere in the process silently reconfigures the
product.

| Lock | Pre-fix behaviour after a poisoning |
|---|---|
| `current_prompt_id` (7 readers) | "no turn is live". Streaming updates lost their `promptId`, so the client could no longer group deltas into the running turn; `turn_running` reported false while a turn was running; `x.ai/runningPromptId` disappeared; and `mark_apply_miss_incomplete` failed *closed* -- an apply-miss landed against no prompt at all, so the ledger that is supposed to stain a broken turn stayed clean |
| the four remote config tiers | the remote tier read as absent, so a lower layer answered: an operator kill-switch turning auto mode, prompt suggestion, remembered approvals or the remote crash handler **off** silently stopped working |
| `TELEMETRY_CLIENT` | telemetry read as "off" in whichever direction each reader checked: product events stopped (`is_enabled`) or session lifecycle events stopped (`is_session_metrics_enabled`) |
| doom-loop collector | `abort_triggers` said "nothing to act on", `disarm_abort` dropped the disarm, `take()` reported an empty drain -- the retry/abort machinery ran on an empty view of a collector that was still filling |
| `FailedResponseCapture` | an armed capture recorded nothing and replayed nothing, so the failed turn lost the recovery it was armed for |
| the tool-actor error tee | the first streaming error was dropped on the write side and the read side took nothing, so a failed stream reported success |
| `CWD_GIT_CACHE` | `cwd_git_info_lazy` returned `None` *and* reserved no slot, so the header stayed empty and no refresh was ever scheduled; `update_from_notification` skipped the update it was handed |

Writers were converted with their readers (`*REMOTE_AUTO_MODE_CONFIG.write_or_recover() = ..`, the collector's
record/disarm/clear, `update_from_notification`, the tee's write arm), because converting only the reader leaves a
lock whose writers can still park it in a state the readers were just told to survive.

== 5. The gate

`scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py`. One regex for the site, then three things a naive version gets wrong:

  * the *receiver* is reconstructed and printed, because `?.lock().ok()` names nothing a reader can act on;
  * the window that the receiver is searched in is cut at `;`, `{` and `}` -- **not** at a newline, which is
    precisely what rustfmt puts inside a long chain. The first version cut at newlines and printed `?` for every
    wrapped site; that bug is fixture `test_a_wrapped_chain_reports_its_receiver_not_a_question_mark`;
  * comments, string and char literals are blanked before matching, and `target/`, `node_modules/`, `third_party/`
    and `__pycache__` are pruned -- with a fixture that keeps one clean file *outside* the pruned directories, so
    the prune test cannot pass by having read nothing at all.

Exit codes: `0` clean, `1` names each site as `crate: rel:line: recv.acq().ok()  |  <source line>`, `2` the root
contains no Rust sources (a tree it cannot read is not a clean tree). `--verbose` prints the number of files
scanned, which is what makes `0 site(s)` distinguishable from "scanned 0 files".

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lock-poison.py --root . --verbose
    lock-poison: scanned 2998 .rs file(s) under /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code
    lock-poison: 0 site(s); no lock error is read as an absent value

Fixtures, `scripts/ci/test-check-lock-poison.py`, 11 cases: the four green shapes of section 3 stay green; each
acquisition is named with its receiver; a `#[cfg(any())]` site is excluded while a live sibling is still reported;
an idiom inside a string or comment is ignored and then flipped live to prove the blanking is not a silencer;
`#[cfg(test)]` code *is* scanned; the vendored/build trees are pruned; a root with no sources exits 2; the shipped
tree is clean.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-lock-poison.py
    Ran 11 tests in 12.999s

    OK

Two of those 11 hold the inventory honest, because an inventory that quietly counts the wrong things is how a wrong
number reaches a TODO row. `test_the_inventory_buckets_the_allowed_branches` plants one site of each shape in one
fixture and asserts all four bucket cells plus the totals, so the line numbers -- not the author's reading -- carry
which `match` arm recovered and which invented absence. `test_the_inventory_counts_prose_only_once_it_becomes_code`
is the same non-vacuity pair the gate uses: the fixture contains `if let Ok(guard) = CACHE.read()` inside a doc
comment and must read 5 rows, and flipping that comment into a function must read 6.

Both were then mutated, splicing the scanner rather than the Rust, and both cells failed the named test and restored
byte-identically:

      detected  I1 drops_the_guard always says drop  restore=ok  failing: test_the_inventory_buckets_the_allowed_branches
      detected  I2 boundary widened to try_lock      restore=ok  failing: test_the_inventory_buckets_the_allowed_branches,
                                                                           test_the_inventory_counts_prose_only_once_it_becomes_code

      2/2 cells detected the broken scanner

`I2` matters more than it looks: widening the acquisition to `try_lock` is the one edit that would make the inventory
grow while every existing fixture stayed green, because "someone else holds it right now" and "the holder died" share
the word lock and nothing else.

Wiring is the pair the `check-guard-wiring` gate enforces (`.github/workflows/ci.yml` and the `gates=()` entry in
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`):

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (89 files in scripts/ci/, 88 reachable, 80 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 8 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)

== 6. The tests

Ten behavior tests, each poisoning the real lock through a thread that dies inside the guard and then driving the
shipped reader:

| Test | What it demands |
|---|---|
| `sampler::doom_loop::a_poisoned_collector_keeps_recording_and_aborting` | signals recorded before the death are still drained, and a disarm written after it still lands |
| `sampler::doom_loop_recovery::a_poisoned_capture_still_replays_the_failed_turn` | an armed capture stays armed and replays both frames of the failed turn, reasoning before assistant |
| `pager::git_info::a_poisoned_cwd_cache_still_serves_and_accepts_updates` | a poisoned cache still serves the known entry, still reserves a refresh slot, and still accepts a notification |
| `telemetry::mode_readers_survive_a_poisoned_client_lock` | in `SessionMetrics`, the lifecycle gate stays open and `is_enabled` does not invent a mode that was never installed |
| `telemetry::product_event_gate_survives_a_poisoned_client_lock` | in `Enabled`, the product-event gate stays open too (added by this batch -- see section 7) |
| four `cached_remote_survives_a_poisoned_lock` | each remote tier still answers from what was installed; `auto_mode`'s copy also compares the resolved config before and after on three fields |
| `shell::apply_miss_matching_pin_stains_prompt_through_a_poisoned_pin` | with the pin poisoned, an apply miss against a matching prompt still returns true and still stains both ledgers |

Poisoning is process-wide, so a leaked poison panics an unrelated sibling test. Every test that touches a global
lock therefore takes that module's existing serializer, restores the cached value in a `Drop` guard, and calls
`clear_poison()`. Three test-side accessors were converted for the same reason -- two `CWD_GIT_CACHE.lock().expect(
"cache lock")` calls and `auto_mode`'s `.read().unwrap()` -- and `xai-grok-telemetry` gained a `CLIENT_TEST_LOCK`
because it had no serializer for the two tests that install the global client.

One claim from the previous batch's open row did not survive this one. That row said these call sites could not be
covered because "no test seam in this repository can construct the precondition" -- a live session actor that has
published a prompt id, with its pin mutex then poisoned. It was written in good faith and it was wrong: the seam was
twenty lines away, in `subagent_usage_fold_tests.rs`, whose existing `make_actor()` builds a real actor, and
`apply_miss_matching_pin_stains_prompt_through_a_poisoned_pin` uses it exactly that way (publish the pin, poison the
lock through a thread that dies inside the guard, then drive `mark_apply_miss_incomplete` and require both usage
ledgers still get stained). The row has been rewritten as partial and now lists the 12 readers that remain, each with
its file and line, instead of declaring them unreachable. It is worth recording why that sentence was expensive: a
claim of impossibility in a ledger is not a caveat, it is a stop sign -- the next reader does not look for the seam.

The poisoning machinery itself is now shared: `xai-grok-test-support::poison` exposes
`mutex_through_a_panicking_thread` and `rwlock_through_a_panicking_writer`, each of which acquires with
`unwrap_or_else(PoisonError::into_inner)` (so only the deliberate `panic!` poisons), wraps the call in
`catch_unwind`, and asserts `is_poisoned()` afterwards. `util::shared_guard`'s two `#[cfg(test)]` helpers delegate
to it instead of carrying their own copies; telemetry keeps a local 6-line copy because it deliberately does not
dev-depend on a crate that links a PTY/WebSocket/HTTP server.

== 7. Mutation matrix, and the cell that found this batch's own hole

13 cells. Each splices the pre-fix text -- `git show HEAD:<file>`, verbatim wherever the pre-fix code fits the
current control flow -- over one converted site, runs the single test written for it, restores the file from a byte
copy and re-reads it to compare sha256. The driver aborts on a non-green baseline, so a green matrix cannot be a
matrix run against a broken tree. Two cells note in their own text that HEAD wrapped the whole body in
`if let Ok(..)`; there the pre-fix *semantic* ("skip the update") is reproduced rather than the exact text.

The first run: **12 of 13**.

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/implementer/b25/mutation-driver.py
    ...
      detected  M7 telemetry is_session_metrics_enabled
      MISSED  M8 telemetry is_enabled
    ...
      M7 telemetry is_session_metrics_enabled  detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: session lifecycle emission stopped
      M8 telemetry is_enabled                  MISSED     restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: product events stopped
      M9 shell crash_handler reader            detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: the remote tier read as absent
      M10 shell tool_approvals reader          detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: a lower layer decided persistence
      M11 shell auto_mode reader               detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: the kill-switch fell to the local layer
      M12 shell prompt_suggest reader          detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: same shape, the suggestion gate
      M13 shell mark_apply_miss pin            detected   restore=ok  HEAD verbatim: fail-closed opened

    12/13 cells detected the pre-fix code

`M8` is right, and the reason is worth keeping: the telemetry test that was supposed to cover `is_enabled` installs
`TelemetryMode::SessionMetrics`, and `is_enabled()` is only true in `Enabled`. In `SessionMetrics` both the pre-fix
reader and the fixed one answer false, so the assertion could not tell a working reader from a broken one. The
conversion was correct; the *test* was vacuous for that one gate, and nothing but the matrix would have said so.
The fix is a second test installed in `Enabled` -- `product_event_gate_survives_a_poisoned_client_lock`, which also
asserts `tokio::runtime::Handle::try_current().is_err()` so that a client reaching Mixpanel panics loudly instead of
passing on nothing -- and `M8` was then re-pointed at it:

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/implementer/b25/mutation-driver.py --only telemetry
    == restricted run: --only telemetry selects 2 cell(s) ==
    == green baseline before any mutation ==
      PASS  xai-grok-telemetry  mode_readers_survive_a_poisoned_client_lock
      PASS  xai-grok-telemetry  product_event_gate_survives_a_poisoned_client_lock
      detected  M7 telemetry is_session_metrics_enabled
      detected  M8 telemetry is_enabled

    2/2 cells detected the pre-fix code

So the honest statement is 13/13 *after* one test was added in response to the matrix, not 13/13 on the first try.
Every cell in both runs restored its file byte-identically (`restore=ok` in all 13 rows).

